\title{One bad associated type binding makes every use of the impl's associated types an error}
\taxon{Problem}
\date{2026-11-15}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{schrodingerzy}
\tag{compiler}
\tag{diagnostics}
\meta{status}{open}
\meta{created}{2026-11-15}
\import{base-macros}

\p{\strong{Status:} open.}
\pre{\verb~|
pub trait Tr { type A; fn f(self: Self) -> Self::A; }
pub struct Q { v: i64 }
impl Tr for Q {
    type A = i64;
    type Extra = bool;
    fn f(self: Q) -> Self::A { self.v }
}
~}
\p{\strong{Symptom.} The expected report for \code{\verb~|Extra~} is followed by \code{\verb~|no associated type `A` on `Self`~} at the method, although \code{\verb~|A~} is declared and bound correctly. A duplicate or a missing binding has the same effect, once per use.}
\p{\strong{Cause.} \code{\verb~|scan_impl_assoc_tys~} returns nothing when any binding is wrong, so the table that \code{\verb~|Self::Name~} is looked up in is empty for the methods of that impl.}
\p{\strong{Direction.} Keep the bindings that are valid when another one is not. Found by the fuzzing of associated types recorded in [ses-TKVW](ses-TKVW).}
